1155-    /// `:reset` left the session in continuation mode with that line still
1156-    /// buffered: the next line was appended to a block nobody was typing any
1157-    /// more, so `say "hi"` waited for an `end` that was never coming and never
1158-    /// ran. A session that has been reset is a session at a prompt, so the buffer
1159-    /// goes with the bindings.
1160-    ///
1161-    /// The recorded definitions go with them, and that half was just as visible.
1162-    /// They are a map from a name to the line that bound it, and a name a reset
1163-    /// session no longer holds was still reported by `.which` as a session
1164-    /// binding at `<session>:3` — a span pointing at a line of a session that had
1165-    /// been thrown away, quoted at that. A reset forgets what it was; a `.which`
1166-    /// that disagrees with it is worse than the stale binding it used to report,
1167-    /// because it is a wrong answer rather than an absence.
1168-    ///
1169-    /// The history is left alone: it is the record of what was typed, and a reset
1170-    /// is not a new session in the sense that the earlier lines were never typed.
